Standalone qtest exerciser for the Mach64 GUI (2D) engine.

Run:
  ./qemu-system-ppc -M g3beige -display none -accel qtest \
      -qtest unix:/tmp/qt.sock,server,nowait -bios <rom> \
      -audiodev none,id=q -global awacs.audiodev=q &
  python3 test_2d.py /tmp/qt.sock

Programs the ATI PCI BARs through the Grackle config window, seeds
VRAM patterns, issues operations through the register interface and
verifies the resulting bytes against independent software models:

  - solid fill, overlapping copy, scissor clipping, 16bpp XOR fill,
    reversed-direction (corner-coordinate) copies
  - host-data blits: documented byte order (bytes low-to-high,
    MSB-first per byte, byte-boundary row alignment), the Mac NDRV's
    HOST_CNTL big-endian translation, transparent (bkgd mix = D)
    expansion, packed 8bpp color and byte-swapped 16bpp color
  - NOT_DST invert; mono 8x8 pattern fill through both programming
    models (Apple's FRGD_SRC_PATTERN + PAT_CNTL bit 24, XFree86's
    MONO_SRC_PATTERN + PAT_CNTL bit 0), documented row packing
    (row 0 = low byte)
  - the full 16-code bitwise mix/ROP table
  - the DP_WRITE_MSK plane mask
  - color-compare transparency, source and destination keyed
  - the Bresenham line engine: 11 lines x all octants x LAST_PEL
    on/off, checked pixel-exact against a software Bresenham using
    the documented register formulas
  - mono expansion from a 1bpp VRAM blit source (MONO_SRC_BLIT)
  - the Rage Pro DP_SET_GUI_ENGINE2 one-write context macro (the way
    Apple's accelerated NDRV programs every operation)
  - GUI_TRAJ_CNTL decomposition into DST/SRC/PAT/HOST_CNTL (the way
    Linux's atyfb programs its engine defaults)

All values are written as little-endian raw bytes ("write" commands)
since the register aperture is a little-endian device. Exits nonzero
on any failure.
